    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is overall very well structured: the new `FiscalYear` class correctly centralizes the fiscal-year rule so the three call sites (mapper label, membership number year segment, tenure count) share one definition instead of duplicating month/year arithmetic. It mirrors the existing sibling model classes (`AgeBand`, `CityRegions`, `LuhnCheckDigit`) exactly \u2014 `final`, private constructor, static methods, \"single definition\"/\"lives beside `Owner`\" javadoc \u2014 and the mapper's `toFiscalYear` follows the established `toAgeBand`/`toCheckDigit` delegation pattern. That part is idiomatic and clean.\n\nOne structural nit to fix:\n\n- `AssignMembership.TENURE_FISCAL_YEARS_FOR_LONG_TENURE` (line 36) is still typed `long`. That type was justified under the old implementation because `ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(...)` returns `long`. Now the value it's compared against, `FiscalYear.elapsedYears(...)`, returns `int`, and the constant represents a small count of fiscal years \u2014 the same domain as the neighboring count constant `LARGE_HOUSEHOLD_SIZE`, which is `int`. Leaving it `long` is a leftover from the days-based version and is inconsistent with the sibling count constants. Change it to `int` so the type matches its new meaning and its neighbors.\n\nEverything else reads as a natural, additive change a maintainer of this codebase would expect.",
    "review_cost_usd": 0.3261,
